# $name

A protocol pack for the Deepfake Workbench (`dfwb`): published dataset protocols, meaning
which videos exist, their labels, and exactly how they are split, with no runtime
dependencies of its own. It never contains media, frames, crops, face boxes or anything
derived from pixels.

## Installing

```
pip install $name
```

Installing it registers `$name` with `dfwb` through the `dfwb.plugins` entry point; run
`dfwb protocols list` afterwards to see what it publishes.

## Adding a dataset

With the dataset's inventory builder installed and a local inventory built, write the dataset's
files from it:

```
dfwb protocols build <dataset_id> --out src/$import/packs/<dataset_id> --update-pack-yaml
```

That writes `dataset.yaml`, `labels.yaml`, `videos.jsonl.gz`, `pairs.jsonl.gz` (when the
dataset has pairs), one `splits/<scheme>.tsv.gz` per scheme, `NOTICE.md` and
`PROVENANCE.json`, and lists `<dataset_id>` under `datasets:` in `src/$import/packs/pack.yaml`.
Once you have reviewed the dataset's terms, record the decision as `distribution` in its
`dataset.yaml` and build again, which rewrites `NOTICE.md` to match:

- `distribution: list`: the terms let the key lists be redistributed, and the pack ships every
  file above.
- `distribution: recipe`: they do not. The folder you publish leaves out `videos.jsonl.gz`,
  `pairs.jsonl.gz` and `splits/`, and ships only `dataset.yaml`, `labels.yaml`, `NOTICE.md` and
  `PROVENANCE.json`. Users rebuild the lists from their own copy of the dataset with
  `dfwb protocols materialize <dataset_id>`, which checks them against the hashes in
  `dataset.yaml`. That needs every video of the release, in each compression the card lists,
  and an inventory built by the builder version `PROVENANCE.json` records.

Run `dfwb protocols lint --release` against the `packs` folder you publish before releasing it.

## Requirements

This pack has no runtime dependencies, so its `pyproject.toml` pins no dfwb version; the
requirement is stated here instead. Cards written by `dfwb protocols build` carry the recipe
hash fields (`videos_sha256`, `pairs_sha256` and `pairing_rule`), which an earlier dfwb cannot
read, so the pack needs a dfwb that reads the recipe hash fields: 0.1.0b3 or later, once
released.

